    (v_topic_id, 'A 74-year-old female with multiple chronic conditions, recurrent falls, and polypharmacy is referred to a Comprehensive Geriatric Assessment (CGA) program. The patient asks why she needs to see a whole team instead of just her primary care doctor. Which of the following statements best describes the evidence-based benefit and composition of the CGA interdisciplinary team?', '["CGA is a home safety inspection team that is legally mandated to confiscate driving licenses from patients over 80", "CGA is a billing code that allows multiple physicians to charge for the same office visit without collaborating", "CGA is a palliative care service designed only for patients with a life expectancy of less than 6 months", "CGA is a diagnostic tool performed exclusively by a neurologist to determine genetic risk profiles for dementia", "CGA utilizes an interdisciplinary team (geriatrician, nurse, pharmacist, social worker, PT/OT) to deliver multidimensional assessment and coordinated care, which has been shown to reduce hospital readmissions, delay nursing home placement, and improve functional survival"]'::jsonb, 4, 'A **Comprehensive Geriatric Assessment (CGA)** is a multidimensional, interdisciplinary diagnostic and therapeutic process designed to determine an older person''s medical, psychological, and functional capabilities. The core interdisciplinary team typically includes a geriatrician, nurse, clinical pharmacist, social worker, and physical/occupational therapists. Strong clinical trial evidence demonstrates that CGA-based care leads to **improved functional status, reduced rates of hospitalization and nursing home placement, and increased likelihood of remaining alive in one''s own home** compared to standard care. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 12; Ellis et al., Cochrane Database Syst Rev 2017)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 92-year-old man is undergoing a Comprehensive Geriatric Assessment (CGA). During the evaluation, the team screens the patient''s physical health, performs cognitive testing (MoCA), reviews all medications, assesses safety in the home environment, screens for depression, evaluates social support networks, and reviews advanced directives. Which of the following describes the key principle underlying the assessment of these multiple domains?', '["The CGA is designed to replace all standard medical treatments with non-pharmacological therapies", "The environmental assessment is the only domain with prognostic value for mortality in older adults", "Physical, cognitive, psychological, social, and environmental domains are highly interdependent in older adults; an unaddressed deficit in one domain (e.g., social isolation) can accelerate decline in another (e.g., cognitive and physical function)", "The assessment of cognitive and psychological domains is only performed if a patient has a formal diagnosis of schizophrenia", "The domains are assessed separately because they do not influence one another, allowing different specialists to work in complete isolation"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The defining feature of the **Comprehensive Geriatric Assessment (CGA)** is its **multidimensionality**. Older adults typically have multiple coexisting problems across physical, cognitive, psychological, social, economic, and environmental domains. These domains are **highly interdependent**—for example, severe osteoarthritis (physical) can lead to restricted mobility, which causes social isolation (social/environmental), triggering depression (psychological), which worsens cognitive testing scores and leads to poor nutrition and medication adherence. Addressing all domains in an integrated fashion is essential for effective care. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 12)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 91-year-old man is undergoing a Comprehensive Geriatric Assessment (CGA). During the evaluation, the team screens the patient''s physical health, performs cognitive testing (MoCA), reviews all medications, assesses safety in the home environment, screens for depression, evaluates social support networks, and reviews advanced directives. Which of the following describes the key principle underlying the assessment of these multiple domains?', '["The environmental assessment is the only domain with prognostic value for mortality in older adults", "Physical, cognitive, psychological, social, and environmental domains are highly interdependent in older adults; an unaddressed deficit in one domain (e.g., social isolation) can accelerate decline in another (e.g., cognitive and physical function)", "The CGA is designed to replace all standard medical treatments with non-pharmacological therapies", "The assessment of cognitive and psychological domains is only performed if a patient has a formal diagnosis of schizophrenia", "The domains are assessed separately because they do not influence one another, allowing different specialists to work in complete isolation"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The defining feature of the **Comprehensive Geriatric Assessment (CGA)** is its **multidimensionality**. Older adults typically have multiple coexisting problems across physical, cognitive, psychological, social, economic, and environmental domains. These domains are **highly interdependent**—for example, severe osteoarthritis (physical) can lead to restricted mobility, which causes social isolation (social/environmental), triggering depression (psychological), which worsens cognitive testing scores and leads to poor nutrition and medication adherence. Addressing all domains in an integrated fashion is essential for effective care. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 12)'),
