    (v_topic_id, 'A health system''s clinical quality team is developing a screening protocol to identify patients who would benefit most from a referral to a high-resource Comprehensive Geriatric Assessment (CGA) clinic for a cohort of patients over age 90. Which of the following patients represents the **most appropriate** target population for a CGA?', '["A 90-year-old male with severe, advanced Alzheimer''s disease who is completely bedbound, non-verbal, and dependent for all basic ADLs", "A 90-year-old male with end-stage metastatic pancreatic cancer who is currently receiving hospice care in a dedicated facility", "A 90-year-old male with mild cognitive decline, progressive gait unsteadiness, recent emergency department visits for minor falls, and polypharmacy (taking 6 medications)", "A 45-year-old patient with new-onset type 1 diabetes and no other medical or functional comorbidities", "A 90-year-old male who is highly active, jogs 3 miles daily, manages all his own finances, and has well-controlled hypertension on one medication"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The **target population** for CGA consists of older adults who are **moderately frail, have complex medical-psychosocial needs, or are at risk of functional decline** but still have **rehabilitative potential**. Robust older adults (who are healthy and independent) do not need the resource-intensive CGA. Conversely, patients with end-stage terminal illness (who are on hospice) or those with advanced, irreversible functional dependency (bedbound, end-stage dementia) do not benefit from the diagnostic and rehabilitative focus of a CGA; their care is better managed through primary palliative/comfort-focused care. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 12; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips)'),
