    (v_topic_id, 'A 67-year-old man is evaluated for general decline. Over the past year, the patient has had an unintentional weight loss of **7 lbs** (his baseline was 130 lbs), reports feeling exhausted ''nearly every day,'' has a slow walking speed (takes 8 seconds to walk 4 meters), has weak grip strength, and reports low physical activity (sedentary lifestyle). According to the **Fried Frailty Phenotype**, how should this patient''s frailty status be classified, and what is the biological basis of this condition?', '["Anorexic; the weight loss is the only clinically significant finding and should be treated with high-dose stimulants", "Pre-frail (satisfies 1-2 criteria); managed primarily with long-term bed rest to conserve energy", "Robust (satisfies 0 criteria); the patient''s symptoms are a normal and inevitable consequence of chronological aging", "Frail (satisfies 3 or more criteria); frailty is a syndrome of physiological vulnerability characterized by chronic low-grade inflammation (inflammaging) and multi-system dysregulation", "Sarcopenic only; the patient does not meet frailty criteria because cognitive function is intact"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The **Fried Frailty Phenotype** defines frailty based on **five clinical criteria**: (1) Unintentional weight loss (≥10 lbs or ≥5% of body weight in the past year); (2) Self-reported exhaustion; (3) Weakness (low grip strength); (4) Slowness (slow walking speed over 4 meters); (5) Low physical activity. Scoring: **0 criteria = Robust**; **1–2 criteria = Pre-frail**; **≥3 criteria = Frail**. This patient meets all five criteria (or at least 3: weight loss, exhaustion, slowness, weakness, low activity), classifying them as **Frail**. Pathophysiologically, frailty represents a state of reduced physiologic reserve and high vulnerability to stressors, driven by **chronic inflammation (''inflammaging'')**, mitochondrial dysfunction, endocrine dysregulation (HPA axis, IGF-1), and sarcopenia. (Ref: Fried et al., J Gerontol A Biol Sci Med Sci 2001; Hazzard''s Ch. 12, Ch. 26)'),
