    (v_topic_id, 'A 97-year-old male is evaluated for general decline. Over the past year, the patient has had an unintentional weight loss of **7 lbs** (his baseline was 130 lbs), reports feeling exhausted ''nearly every day,'' has a slow walking speed (takes 8 seconds to walk 4 meters), has weak grip strength, and reports low physical activity (sedentary lifestyle). According to the **Fried Frailty Phenotype**, how should this patient''s frailty status be classified, and what is the biological basis of this condition?', '["Robust (satisfies 0 criteria); the patient''s symptoms are a normal and inevitable consequence of chronological aging", "Frail (satisfies 3 or more criteria); frailty is a syndrome of physiological vulnerability characterized by chronic low-grade inflammation (inflammaging) and multi-system dysregulation", "Anorexic; the weight loss is the only clinically significant finding and should be treated with high-dose stimulants", "Sarcopenic only; the patient does not meet frailty criteria because cognitive function is intact", "Pre-frail (satisfies 1-2 criteria); managed primarily with long-term bed rest to conserve energy"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The **Fried Frailty Phenotype** defines frailty based on **five clinical criteria**: (1) Unintentional weight loss (≥10 lbs or ≥5% of body weight in the past year); (2) Self-reported exhaustion; (3) Weakness (low grip strength); (4) Slowness (slow walking speed over 4 meters); (5) Low physical activity. Scoring: **0 criteria = Robust**; **1–2 criteria = Pre-frail**; **≥3 criteria = Frail**. This patient meets all five criteria (or at least 3: weight loss, exhaustion, slowness, weakness, low activity), classifying them as **Frail**. Pathophysiologically, frailty represents a state of reduced physiologic reserve and high vulnerability to stressors, driven by **chronic inflammation (''inflammaging'')**, mitochondrial dysfunction, endocrine dysregulation (HPA axis, IGF-1), and sarcopenia. (Ref: Fried et al., J Gerontol A Biol Sci Med Sci 2001; Hazzard''s Ch. 12, Ch. 26)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 85-year-old woman is scheduled for an elective total hip arthroplasty. During the pre-operative assessment, the clinician utilizes the **Clinical Frailty Scale (CFS)**. The patient is found to be active but has progressive difficulty with complex IADLs (requires assistance with shopping and heavy housework), though she is fully independent in all basic ADLs (DEATH). Which of the following CFS scores is most appropriate, and what does it predict?', '["CFS 4 (Vulnerable) or CFS 5 (Mildly Frail); indicates that the patient is at an increased risk of post-operative delirium, prolonged hospital stay, and discharge to an inpatient rehabilitation facility", "CFS 3 (Managing Well); indicates that the patient has no active medical problems or physical limitations", "CFS 1 (Very Fit); predicts a flawless post-operative course with zero risk of surgical complications", "CFS 7 (Severely Frail); indicates that the patient is completely dependent for all self-care tasks", "CFS 9 (Terminally Ill); indicates that elective surgery is absolutely contraindicated and the patient should be transitioned to hospice"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The **Clinical Frailty Scale (CFS)** is a 9-point visual scale used to rapidly assess frailty. **CFS 4 (Vulnerable)** describes patients who are not dependent but are limited in activities and commonly complain of ''slowing down''. **CFS 5 (Mildly Frail)** describes patients with more evident slowing who require help with complex IADLs (finances, transportation, heavy housework) but maintain independent basic ADLs. In pre-operative settings, a CFS score of 4 or 5 (or higher) is a powerful predictor of adverse outcomes: **increased risk of post-operative delirium, surgical complications, increased length of stay, and institutional discharge** (discharge to rehab). Identifying frailty pre-operatively allows for targeted interventions (prehabilitation, geriatric co-management). (Ref: Rockwood et al., CMAJ 2005; Hazzard''s Ch. 12, Ch. 26)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 89-year-old female is scheduled for an elective total hip arthroplasty. During the pre-operative assessment, the clinician utilizes the **Clinical Frailty Scale (CFS)**. The patient is found to be active but has progressive difficulty with complex IADLs (requires assistance with shopping and heavy housework), though she is fully independent in all basic ADLs (DEATH). Which of the following CFS scores is most appropriate, and what does it predict?', '["CFS 9 (Terminally Ill); indicates that elective surgery is absolutely contraindicated and the patient should be transitioned to hospice", "CFS 1 (Very Fit); predicts a flawless post-operative course with zero risk of surgical complications", "CFS 4 (Vulnerable) or CFS 5 (Mildly Frail); indicates that the patient is at an increased risk of post-operative delirium, prolonged hospital stay, and discharge to an inpatient rehabilitation facility", "CFS 3 (Managing Well); indicates that the patient has no active medical problems or physical limitations", "CFS 7 (Severely Frail); indicates that the patient is completely dependent for all self-care tasks"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The **Clinical Frailty Scale (CFS)** is a 9-point visual scale used to rapidly assess frailty. **CFS 4 (Vulnerable)** describes patients who are not dependent but are limited in activities and commonly complain of ''slowing down''. **CFS 5 (Mildly Frail)** describes patients with more evident slowing who require help with complex IADLs (finances, transportation, heavy housework) but maintain independent basic ADLs. In pre-operative settings, a CFS score of 4 or 5 (or higher) is a powerful predictor of adverse outcomes: **increased risk of post-operative delirium, surgical complications, increased length of stay, and institutional discharge** (discharge to rehab). Identifying frailty pre-operatively allows for targeted interventions (prehabilitation, geriatric co-management). (Ref: Rockwood et al., CMAJ 2005; Hazzard''s Ch. 12, Ch. 26)'),
