    (v_topic_id, 'A 88-year-old male is scheduled for an elective total hip arthroplasty. During the pre-operative assessment, the clinician utilizes the **Clinical Frailty Scale (CFS)**. The patient is found to be active but has progressive difficulty with complex IADLs (requires assistance with shopping and heavy housework), though he is fully independent in all basic ADLs (DEATH). Which of the following CFS scores is most appropriate, and what does it predict?', '["CFS 4 (Vulnerable) or CFS 5 (Mildly Frail); indicates that the patient is at an increased risk of post-operative delirium, prolonged hospital stay, and discharge to an inpatient rehabilitation facility", "CFS 3 (Managing Well); indicates that the patient has no active medical problems or physical limitations", "CFS 1 (Very Fit); predicts a flawless post-operative course with zero risk of surgical complications", "CFS 9 (Terminally Ill); indicates that elective surgery is absolutely contraindicated and the patient should be transitioned to hospice", "CFS 7 (Severely Frail); indicates that the patient is completely dependent for all self-care tasks"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The **Clinical Frailty Scale (CFS)** is a 9-point visual scale used to rapidly assess frailty. **CFS 4 (Vulnerable)** describes patients who are not dependent but are limited in activities and commonly complain of ''slowing down''. **CFS 5 (Mildly Frail)** describes patients with more evident slowing who require help with complex IADLs (finances, transportation, heavy housework) but maintain independent basic ADLs. In pre-operative settings, a CFS score of 4 or 5 (or higher) is a powerful predictor of adverse outcomes: **increased risk of post-operative delirium, surgical complications, increased length of stay, and institutional discharge** (discharge to rehab). Identifying frailty pre-operatively allows for targeted interventions (prehabilitation, geriatric co-management). (Ref: Rockwood et al., CMAJ 2005; Hazzard''s Ch. 12, Ch. 26)'),
