    (v_topic_id, 'A 97-year-old woman with a history of stage III colon cancer is being evaluated for adjuvant chemotherapy. The oncologist is hesitant to prescribe a standard multi-agent regimen due to concerns about toxicity. A formal frailty assessment reveals that the patient is moderately frail. Which of the following statements regarding the clinical implications of frailty in oncology and surgery is most accurate?', '["Frailty is a strong independent predictor of severe chemotherapeutic toxicity, post-operative complications, and mortality, regardless of chronological age; treatment plans should be tailored based on physiological age rather than chronological age", "Frail patients should always receive the highest possible dose of chemotherapy to overcome their physiological vulnerability", "Chronological age is a far superior predictor of chemotherapy tolerability and surgical outcomes than frailty scoring", "Frailty scores are highly subjective and have not been validated in clinical oncology or surgical outcomes research", "Frailty is only relevant for musculoskeletal procedures and has no predictive value for systemic cancer therapies"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Frailty is a measure of **physiological age and vulnerability**, which is distinct from and far superior to chronological age in predicting clinical outcomes. In both surgical and oncology settings, frailty is a powerful, validated predictor of adverse outcomes: it independently predicts **grade 3/4 chemotherapeutic toxicity, treatment discontinuation, post-operative complications (infections, wound dehiscence), prolonged hospitalization, and short-term mortality**. Consequently, international guidelines recommend screening for frailty in older adults before starting chemotherapy or undergoing major surgery, allowing clinicians to tailor treatment (e.g., using dose-adjusted regimens or choosing less invasive surgical options). (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 26, Ch. 99; Hurria et al., J Clin Oncol 2011)'),
