    (v_topic_id, 'A 73-year-old man is diagnosed with frailty syndrome. The patient''s family asks about the underlying biological mechanisms that lead to this state of extreme physical vulnerability. Which of the following best describes the core pathophysiological features of frailty?', '["Chronic vitamin D toxicity leading to hypercalcemic muscle paralysis and severe metastatic calcification of the joints", "Multi-system physiological dysregulation driven by chronic, low-grade inflammation (elevated IL-6, TNF-alpha, CRP), dysregulation of the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis, decreased anabolic hormones (IGF-1, DHEA-S), mitochondrial dysfunction, and skeletal muscle loss (sarcopenia)", "Selective autoimmune destruction of peripheral myelinated motor axons, similar to Guillain-Barr\u00e9 syndrome", "A single genetic mutation in the NOTCH3 gene that leads to selective atrophy of the cerebral cortex and motor neurons", "Primary psychiatric disease characterized by voluntary muscle disuse and secondary psychogenic muscle wasting"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Frailty is not caused by a single organ failure or a single disease; it is a **multi-system physiological syndrome** driven by the accumulation of subclinical declines across multiple organ systems. The core pathophysiological drivers include: (1) **''Inflammaging''** — chronic, sterile, low-grade inflammation with elevated levels of pro-inflammatory cytokines (IL-6, TNF-alpha) and C-reactive protein (CRP); (2) **Endocrine dysregulation** — blunted growth hormone/IGF-1 axis, reduced DHEA-S, and chronic activation or blunting of the HPA axis (high cortisol); (3) **Mitochondrial dysfunction** and oxidative stress; (4) **Sarcopenia** — the age-related loss of skeletal muscle mass, quality, and strength. These pathways interact in a vicious cycle leading to reduced energy production, weight loss, and physical vulnerability. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 26; Clegg et al., Lancet 2013)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 75-year-old female is diagnosed with frailty syndrome. The patient''s family asks about the underlying biological mechanisms that lead to this state of extreme physical vulnerability. Which of the following best describes the core pathophysiological features of frailty?', '["A single genetic mutation in the NOTCH3 gene that leads to selective atrophy of the cerebral cortex and motor neurons", "Primary psychiatric disease characterized by voluntary muscle disuse and secondary psychogenic muscle wasting", "Multi-system physiological dysregulation driven by chronic, low-grade inflammation (elevated IL-6, TNF-alpha, CRP), dysregulation of the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis, decreased anabolic hormones (IGF-1, DHEA-S), mitochondrial dysfunction, and skeletal muscle loss (sarcopenia)", "Selective autoimmune destruction of peripheral myelinated motor axons, similar to Guillain-Barr\u00e9 syndrome", "Chronic vitamin D toxicity leading to hypercalcemic muscle paralysis and severe metastatic calcification of the joints"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Frailty is not caused by a single organ failure or a single disease; it is a **multi-system physiological syndrome** driven by the accumulation of subclinical declines across multiple organ systems. The core pathophysiological drivers include: (1) **''Inflammaging''** — chronic, sterile, low-grade inflammation with elevated levels of pro-inflammatory cytokines (IL-6, TNF-alpha) and C-reactive protein (CRP); (2) **Endocrine dysregulation** — blunted growth hormone/IGF-1 axis, reduced DHEA-S, and chronic activation or blunting of the HPA axis (high cortisol); (3) **Mitochondrial dysfunction** and oxidative stress; (4) **Sarcopenia** — the age-related loss of skeletal muscle mass, quality, and strength. These pathways interact in a vicious cycle leading to reduced energy production, weight loss, and physical vulnerability. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 26; Clegg et al., Lancet 2013)'),
