    (v_topic_id, 'During a Comprehensive Geriatric Assessment, a 77-year-old male performs the **Timed Up and Go (TUG)** test. The patient rises from a standard chair, walks 3 meters (10 feet), turns around, walks back to the chair, and sits down. The patient completes this in **19 seconds** using a cane. Which of the following is the correct clinical interpretation of this result?', '["The TUG time is \u226512 seconds (or \u226513.5 seconds depending on criteria), indicating a high risk of falls; the clinician should conduct a detailed multi-factorial fall risk assessment including gait, balance, home safety, and medication review", "The TUG score indicates acute joint infection; prescribe immediate empiric intravenous antibiotics", "The TUG score suggests severe cognitive impairment; initiate immediate referral for a brain MRI and lumbar puncture", "The TUG time is normal, indicating an exceptionally low risk of falls; reassure the patient that no further mobility interventions are needed", "The test is invalid because the patient used a cane; the TUG must only be performed without any assistive devices"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The **Timed Up and Go (TUG)** test is a quick, widely used screening tool for gait and balance impairment. The patient is instructed to stand up from a chair, walk 3 meters, turn, walk back, and sit down. They may use their usual walking aid (e.g., cane, walker). Timing thresholds: **<10 seconds is normal** (independent mobility); **≥12 seconds** (according to AGS/CDC STEADI guidelines) or **≥13.5 seconds** (according to other validated cohorts) indicates **high fall risk**. Because this patient took 19 seconds, they are at high risk of falls. This must trigger a multifactorial fall assessment: reviewing medications (Beers list), checking orthostatic vitals, evaluating vision, assessing home safety (OT), and prescribing physical therapy for balance/strength training. (Ref: AGS/BGS Clinical Practice Guideline for Prevention of Falls; CDC STEADI Tool)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'During a Comprehensive Geriatric Assessment, a 81-year-old man performs the **Timed Up and Go (TUG)** test. The patient rises from a standard chair, walks 3 meters (10 feet), turns around, walks back to the chair, and sits down. The patient completes this in **25 seconds** using a cane. Which of the following is the correct clinical interpretation of this result?', '["The TUG score suggests severe cognitive impairment; initiate immediate referral for a brain MRI and lumbar puncture", "The TUG time is normal, indicating an exceptionally low risk of falls; reassure the patient that no further mobility interventions are needed", "The TUG score indicates acute joint infection; prescribe immediate empiric intravenous antibiotics", "The TUG time is \u226512 seconds (or \u226513.5 seconds depending on criteria), indicating a high risk of falls; the clinician should conduct a detailed multi-factorial fall risk assessment including gait, balance, home safety, and medication review", "The test is invalid because the patient used a cane; the TUG must only be performed without any assistive devices"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The **Timed Up and Go (TUG)** test is a quick, widely used screening tool for gait and balance impairment. The patient is instructed to stand up from a chair, walk 3 meters, turn, walk back, and sit down. They may use their usual walking aid (e.g., cane, walker). Timing thresholds: **<10 seconds is normal** (independent mobility); **≥12 seconds** (according to AGS/CDC STEADI guidelines) or **≥13.5 seconds** (according to other validated cohorts) indicates **high fall risk**. Because this patient took 25 seconds, they are at high risk of falls. This must trigger a multifactorial fall assessment: reviewing medications (Beers list), checking orthostatic vitals, evaluating vision, assessing home safety (OT), and prescribing physical therapy for balance/strength training. (Ref: AGS/BGS Clinical Practice Guideline for Prevention of Falls; CDC STEADI Tool)'),
