    (v_topic_id, 'During a Comprehensive Geriatric Assessment, a 66-year-old female performs the **Timed Up and Go (TUG)** test. The patient rises from a standard chair, walks 3 meters (10 feet), turns around, walks back to the chair, and sits down. The patient completes this in **16 seconds** using a cane. Which of the following is the correct clinical interpretation of this result?', '["The test is invalid because the patient used a cane; the TUG must only be performed without any assistive devices", "The TUG time is normal, indicating an exceptionally low risk of falls; reassure the patient that no further mobility interventions are needed", "The TUG score suggests severe cognitive impairment; initiate immediate referral for a brain MRI and lumbar puncture", "The TUG score indicates acute joint infection; prescribe immediate empiric intravenous antibiotics", "The TUG time is \u226512 seconds (or \u226513.5 seconds depending on criteria), indicating a high risk of falls; the clinician should conduct a detailed multi-factorial fall risk assessment including gait, balance, home safety, and medication review"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The **Timed Up and Go (TUG)** test is a quick, widely used screening tool for gait and balance impairment. The patient is instructed to stand up from a chair, walk 3 meters, turn, walk back, and sit down. They may use their usual walking aid (e.g., cane, walker). Timing thresholds: **<10 seconds is normal** (independent mobility); **≥12 seconds** (according to AGS/CDC STEADI guidelines) or **≥13.5 seconds** (according to other validated cohorts) indicates **high fall risk**. Because this patient took 16 seconds, they are at high risk of falls. This must trigger a multifactorial fall assessment: reviewing medications (Beers list), checking orthostatic vitals, evaluating vision, assessing home safety (OT), and prescribing physical therapy for balance/strength training. (Ref: AGS/BGS Clinical Practice Guideline for Prevention of Falls; CDC STEADI Tool)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'During a Comprehensive Geriatric Assessment, a 74-year-old female performs the **Timed Up and Go (TUG)** test. The patient rises from a standard chair, walks 3 meters (10 feet), turns around, walks back to the chair, and sits down. The patient completes this in **13 seconds** using a cane. Which of the following is the correct clinical interpretation of this result?', '["The TUG score indicates acute joint infection; prescribe immediate empiric intravenous antibiotics", "The TUG time is normal, indicating an exceptionally low risk of falls; reassure the patient that no further mobility interventions are needed", "The test is invalid because the patient used a cane; the TUG must only be performed without any assistive devices", "The TUG score suggests severe cognitive impairment; initiate immediate referral for a brain MRI and lumbar puncture", "The TUG time is \u226512 seconds (or \u226513.5 seconds depending on criteria), indicating a high risk of falls; the clinician should conduct a detailed multi-factorial fall risk assessment including gait, balance, home safety, and medication review"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The **Timed Up and Go (TUG)** test is a quick, widely used screening tool for gait and balance impairment. The patient is instructed to stand up from a chair, walk 3 meters, turn, walk back, and sit down. They may use their usual walking aid (e.g., cane, walker). Timing thresholds: **<10 seconds is normal** (independent mobility); **≥12 seconds** (according to AGS/CDC STEADI guidelines) or **≥13.5 seconds** (according to other validated cohorts) indicates **high fall risk**. Because this patient took 13 seconds, they are at high risk of falls. This must trigger a multifactorial fall assessment: reviewing medications (Beers list), checking orthostatic vitals, evaluating vision, assessing home safety (OT), and prescribing physical therapy for balance/strength training. (Ref: AGS/BGS Clinical Practice Guideline for Prevention of Falls; CDC STEADI Tool)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'During a Comprehensive Geriatric Assessment, a 77-year-old woman performs the **Timed Up and Go (TUG)** test. The patient rises from a standard chair, walks 3 meters (10 feet), turns around, walks back to the chair, and sits down. The patient completes this in **17 seconds** using a cane. Which of the following is the correct clinical interpretation of this result?', '["The TUG time is \u226512 seconds (or \u226513.5 seconds depending on criteria), indicating a high risk of falls; the clinician should conduct a detailed multi-factorial fall risk assessment including gait, balance, home safety, and medication review", "The TUG time is normal, indicating an exceptionally low risk of falls; reassure the patient that no further mobility interventions are needed", "The test is invalid because the patient used a cane; the TUG must only be performed without any assistive devices", "The TUG score indicates acute joint infection; prescribe immediate empiric intravenous antibiotics", "The TUG score suggests severe cognitive impairment; initiate immediate referral for a brain MRI and lumbar puncture"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The **Timed Up and Go (TUG)** test is a quick, widely used screening tool for gait and balance impairment. The patient is instructed to stand up from a chair, walk 3 meters, turn, walk back, and sit down. They may use their usual walking aid (e.g., cane, walker). Timing thresholds: **<10 seconds is normal** (independent mobility); **≥12 seconds** (according to AGS/CDC STEADI guidelines) or **≥13.5 seconds** (according to other validated cohorts) indicates **high fall risk**. Because this patient took 17 seconds, they are at high risk of falls. This must trigger a multifactorial fall assessment: reviewing medications (Beers list), checking orthostatic vitals, evaluating vision, assessing home safety (OT), and prescribing physical therapy for balance/strength training. (Ref: AGS/BGS Clinical Practice Guideline for Prevention of Falls; CDC STEADI Tool)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 69-year-old female with a history of two falls in the past 6 months is evaluated in a physical therapy clinic. The therapist administers the **Berg Balance Scale (BBS)**, which consists of 14 static and dynamic balance tasks. The patient receives a score of **41 out of 56**. Which of the following is the most accurate clinical interpretation and management plan?', '["A score of \u226445 out of 56 indicates a significantly increased risk of recurrent falls; recommend structured balance and strength training (e.g., Otago Exercise Program or physical therapy) and home environmental safety modifications", "A score of 41/56 is in the optimal range, confirming that the patient''s balance is entirely normal and no interventions are required", "The patient requires an immediate orthopedic consultation for bilateral total knee replacements based solely on this score", "A low Berg score is a pathognomonic marker for cerebellar stroke; immediate transfer to the emergency department for stroke activation is indicated", "The Berg Balance Scale is a diagnostic test for Parkinson''s disease; a score of 41 confirms idiopathic Parkinson''s disease"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The **Berg Balance Scale (BBS)** is a 14-item scale designed to measure balance in older adults by assessing performance in functional tasks (such as standing on one foot, reaching forward, turning 360 degrees). The maximum score is 56. A score of **≤45/56 is the classic threshold indicating a high risk of falls**. Because this patient scored 41/56, they are at elevated risk for recurrent falls. The most appropriate management includes: (1) Referral to physical therapy for targeted balance, gait, and strength training; (2) A home safety evaluation (OT); (3) Multi-factorial fall risk reduction (addressing vision, orthostatic hypotension, and polypharmacy). (Ref: Berg et al., Physiother Can 1989; Hazzard''s Ch. 12, Ch. 24)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 88-year-old woman with a history of two falls in the past 6 months is evaluated in a physical therapy clinic. The therapist administers the **Berg Balance Scale (BBS)**, which consists of 14 static and dynamic balance tasks. The patient receives a score of **28 out of 56**. Which of the following is the most accurate clinical interpretation and management plan?', '["A score of 28/56 is in the optimal range, confirming that the patient''s balance is entirely normal and no interventions are required", "A score of \u226445 out of 56 indicates a significantly increased risk of recurrent falls; recommend structured balance and strength training (e.g., Otago Exercise Program or physical therapy) and home environmental safety modifications", "The patient requires an immediate orthopedic consultation for bilateral total knee replacements based solely on this score", "The Berg Balance Scale is a diagnostic test for Parkinson''s disease; a score of 28 confirms idiopathic Parkinson''s disease", "A low Berg score is a pathognomonic marker for cerebellar stroke; immediate transfer to the emergency department for stroke activation is indicated"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The **Berg Balance Scale (BBS)** is a 14-item scale designed to measure balance in older adults by assessing performance in functional tasks (such as standing on one foot, reaching forward, turning 360 degrees). The maximum score is 56. A score of **≤45/56 is the classic threshold indicating a high risk of falls**. Because this patient scored 28/56, they are at elevated risk for recurrent falls. The most appropriate management includes: (1) Referral to physical therapy for targeted balance, gait, and strength training; (2) A home safety evaluation (OT); (3) Multi-factorial fall risk reduction (addressing vision, orthostatic hypotension, and polypharmacy). (Ref: Berg et al., Physiother Can 1989; Hazzard''s Ch. 12, Ch. 24)'),
